<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solidarity (Polish trade union)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Solidarność</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Solidarity is a Polish trade union that emerged at the Lenin Shipyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> in August 1980, the first independent union in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> country to be recognised by the state. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a>, it had 10 million members at its peak in September 1981, a third of the working-age population. The government tried to destroy it with martial law from December 1981, but it survived underground. In the elections agreed at the Round Table talks of 1989 it won 99 of the 100 Senate seats, and a Solidarity-led government followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union.jpg" alt="Solidarity (Polish trade union)" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zenon Mirota · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strajk_sierpniowy_w_Stoczni_Gda%C5%84skiej_im._Lenina_34.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 1970s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s government raised food prices while wages stagnated, and protests in 1976 were followed by a crackdown on dissent. Groups such as KOR and ROPCiO began to form underground networks to monitor and oppose the government, with labour unions an important part of them. In 1979 the Polish economy shrank for the first time since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, and foreign debt reached around 18 billion dollars by 1980. The writings of the philosopher Leszek Kołakowski, banned in Poland, circulated in underground copies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a>'s 1979 visit to Poland was, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a>'s words, a significant factor in the creation of the union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-walentynowicz">Anna Walentynowicz</a> was dismissed from the Gdańsk Shipyard on 7 August 1980, five months before she was due to retire, for taking part in the illegal trade union. Workers struck in her defence on 14 August, and she and Alina Pienkowska turned a strike over bread and butter into a solidarity strike with other establishments. The government signed the agreement allowing the union to exist on 31 August 1980. On 17 September more than twenty founding committees merged into one national organisation, and it was registered on 10 November 1980.</p><p>Solidarity brought together people linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and members of the anti-Soviet left, and it advocated non-violence. In September 1981 its first national congress elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Wałęsa</a> president and adopted a programme called the Self-governing Republic. The government imposed martial law on 13 December 1981 under General Wojciech Jaruzelski, arresting the regional delegates. The union survived underground with financial support from the Vatican and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>The Round Table talks of 6 February to 5 April 1989 led to semi-free elections on 4 June 1989, in which the opposition won 99 of the 100 Senate seats and all 161 contestable seats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>. A Solidarity-led coalition government was formed by the end of August, with Tadeusz Mazowiecki as prime minister, and in December 1990 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Wałęsa</a> was elected President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The union's survival was unprecedented in the Eastern bloc, where Soviet-led invasions had quelled the Hungarian Uprising of 1956 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> of 1968. Its electoral victory in 1989 set off a mostly bloodless chain of events across Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">Revolutions of 1989</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Wałęsa</a> was awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1983. Organised as one big union by region rather than by craft, the union reached about 10 million members in September 1981, a third of the working-age population. After 1989 it became a more traditional trade union, and a political arm, Solidarity Electoral Action, won the parliamentary election of 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solidarity (Polish trade union)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1980 a strike at the Lenin Shipyard in Gdańsk won the right to form independent unions; <b>Solidarity</b>, founded that September as the first independent union recognised in the Soviet bloc, soon had about ten million members.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>At its height <b>Solidarity</b> had over 10 million members, the largest union membership in the world, and the philosopher Leszek Kołakowski called it a working-class revolution “directed against a socialist state, and carried out under the sign of the cross, with the blessing of the Pope.”</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Manmohan Singh recited its first line in his Budget Speech of 1992 in the Lok Sabha, and in recent years it has been taken up by mass movements such as the anti-CAA protests in India and the Pakistani Students <b>Solidarity</b> March.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sarfaroshi-ki-tamanna">Sarfaroshi Ki Tamanna</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Solidaritätslied&quot; (<b>Solidarity</b> Song) is a revolutionary workers' song written between 1929 and 1931 by Bertolt Brecht and set to music by Hanns Eisler.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidaritatslied">Solidaritätslied</a></li><li><blockquote>Chaplin was a committed member of the Industrial Workers of the World, the &quot;Wobblies&quot;, and wrote for <b>Solidarity</b>, the union's paper in the eastern United States, and drew its cartoons.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Solidarity</b> won all but one of the freely contested seats.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
